1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,700
3Sum — LeetCode 15

2
00:00:01,700 --> 00:00:06,000
Problem: find every unique triplet that sums to zero — no duplicate triplets.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:10,200
Example nums = [-1,0,1,2,-1,-4]. The answers are {-1,-1,2} and {-1,0,1}.

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,500
Key idea: sort first — it unlocks everything.

5
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:18,000
Fix one number, then it's just Two Sum on the rest: two pointers chasing minus that number.

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
And skip repeated values so the triplets stay unique.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:25,500
Sorted, it's [-4,-1,-1,0,1,2]. Fix -4 first: no two later numbers reach 4.

8
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Fix -1: we need two summing to 1. -1 and 2 gives 0 — first triplet, {-1,-1,2}.

9
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,500
Then 0 and 1 also sum to 1 with the -1 — second triplet, {-1,0,1}.

10
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:38,500
The next -1 is a repeat of the one we just used, so we skip it — no duplicate triplets.

11
00:00:38,500 --> 00:00:42,000
Fixing 0, the smallest pair already overshoots, so we're done.

12
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,500
Two triplets. Sorting plus a two-pointer sweep per element: O(n²) time, O(1) space.

13
00:00:46,500 --> 00:00:51,000
That wraps the Two Pointers section. AxioByte — follow for all 150.
